<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      Activity recognition. The runtime permission below is API 29+; on 26-28
      the Play services permission is the one that applies, and is granted at
      install. Both are declared so the app behaves the same across minSdk 26.

      This is the single most sensitive thing Harbor asks for, and the
      permission explainer screen has to earn it before the system dialog
      appears. See docs/00-product.md, "Known risks".
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACTIVITY_RECOGNITION" />
    <uses-permission android:name="com.google.android.gms.permission.ACTIVITY_RECOGNITION" />

    <!--
      The scrolling trigger: how long you have been in one app.

      Not a runtime permission and not one an app may be granted the ordinary
      way — it is signature-level, and the only way in is the user finding
      Harbor in Settings > Usage access and turning it on. tools:ignore is
      therefore not a shortcut round a check; the check is a person, in
      Settings, doing it deliberately. sensing/ScrollWatch.hasPermission asks
      AppOps whether they did.

      Declared for everybody and used by almost nobody: it does nothing at all
      unless UserSettings.scrollCues is on, which is off until somebody takes
      the option on the onboarding screen. The disclosure they are shown there
      is written against ScrollWatch, and says what this reads: which app is
      in front, and for how long. There is no API here for anything on the
      screen, and Harbor asks for none of the ones that come close.
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.PACKAGE_USAGE_STATS"
        tools:ignore="ProtectedPermissions" />

    <!-- Re-register transition updates after a reboot; they do not survive it. -->
    <!--
      Placing the call itself, rather than handing the number to the dialer
      for the user to press the green button. Reverses the 2026-09-10
      amendment to ADR-002; see that ADR for what the reversal costs.

      READ_PHONE_STATE is deliberately still absent. Harbor does not watch the
      call, it times how long you were away.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CALL_PHONE" />

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED" />

    <!-- The cue itself. POST_NOTIFICATIONS is a runtime prompt on API 33+. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!--
      Android 14 restricts this to calling and alarm apps, and Harbor hands off
      to the dialer rather than placing calls, so it may not be granted. That
      is survivable: the system degrades a full-screen intent to a heads-up
      notification, which still rings with the contact's sound and still opens
      the surface when tapped. Never treat a refusal as a reason to suppress
      the cue. See ADR-009.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.USE_FULL_SCREEN_INTENT" />

    <!--
      Harbor has no foreground service (ADR-008): it waits as a cached process
      for Play services to wake it with a transition. OEM power management
      freezes exactly that kind of process, and a frozen app is never handed
      the transition — the walk is sensed and nothing runs. Measured on a
      Galaxy S24+ (One UI, Android 16) on 2026-09-17: frozen roughly two
      minutes after being backgrounded, three real walks lost, no error
      anywhere for the app to report.

      Declared so onboarding can offer the system's own one-tap dialog instead
      of sending somebody hunting through Settings. It is asked for, never
      assumed, and a refusal leaves Harbor working exactly as it does today.

      The cost: Play policy restricts this permission to apps whose core
      function genuinely requires it, so a store listing would have to justify
      it. Harbor is built for a one-week study and is not listed. Revisit if
      that ever changes.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.REQUEST_IGNORE_BATTERY_OPTIMIZATIONS" />

    <!--
      The reversal of ADR-008, recorded there and reasoned about in
      sensing/SensingService. Short version: a cached process is frozen by
      every OEM skin this study will meet, and a frozen process is never
      handed the transition, so the cue could not fire at all. A foreground
      service is the only thing that reliably stops a process being cached.

      HEALTH is the type that covers activity recognition, and the runtime
      permission it requires is ACTIVITY_RECOGNITION, which Harbor already
      holds and already explains. It grants nothing new.
    -->
    <!--
      Putting the cue in front of the app somebody is using.

      A full-screen intent covers the locked or dark phone and does nothing
      on one that is unlocked and in use: there the system shows a heads-up
      banner and leaves you where you were. That is right for almost
      everything and wrong for the scrolling trigger, whose entire job is to
      interrupt the feed in front of you. A banner over a feed is a thing
      people flick away without reading.

      This is the only grant that lets an activity start from the background
      on a phone in somebody's hand. It is heavy, it has a frightening name,
      and it is the permission every overlay scam needs, so Harbor asks late
      and only of people who turned the scrolling trigger on. Without it the
      cue degrades to the banner, which is what every other path already
      does; it is never a condition of the app working. See
      cue/CueNotifier.canOpenOverApps.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW" />

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_HEALTH" />

    <!--
      The network. Added 17 Sep 2026 by ADR-013, and worth pausing over.

      Its absence used to be the strongest privacy guarantee in this app: not a
      policy but a fact, checkable by reading this file, that nothing it
      recorded could reach anybody. That guarantee is spent now, because two
      accounts cannot share a schedule through a preferences file and the other
      person has the app too.

      What it buys is exactly one thing so far: asking somebody if you may see
      when they are free, and being told. What it must never buy is the cue
      path. Sensing, the policy, the ledger and the reminder surface all still
      have to work with the radio off, on a train, with no signal. That was
      ADR-003's real argument and it survives the reversal as a constraint.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />

    <!--
      Which other apps Harbor is allowed to see the existence of.

      From Android 11 an app sees only itself and a short list of system
      packages unless it says otherwise here, and `targetSdk` is 37, so the
      filtering is fully on. Without this block the scrolling trigger
      compiles, installs, senses correctly and then never fires: usage access
      lets Harbor read that `com.instagram.android` was in front, but
      `getLaunchIntentForPackage` on that name returns null, and
      ScrollWatch.counts reads a null launch intent as "not an app anybody
      chose to be in". Every real app would be excluded and the trigger would
      be silent for the whole study, with nothing anywhere saying why.
      Confirmed empty on the test phone before this was added.

      The LAUNCHER filter rather than QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES, and the difference
      is the point: this reveals exactly the apps that appear in the app
      drawer, which is exactly the set the trigger cares about. Anything
      without a launcher entry - system UI, the keyguard, the overlays that
      can be reported as foreground - stays invisible, and stays invisible is
      the same answer as excluded. QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES would grant more, is
      restricted on Play, and would be a larger claim than the onboarding
      screen makes.

      HOME is separate because a launcher's home activity is not always also
      a drawer entry, and ScrollWatch has to recognise the launcher in order
      to not count sitting on a home screen as a sitting.

      USAGE_ACCESS_SETTINGS is resolved before the "Open usage access" button
      is offered. Unresolvable means no button, which on a phone where the
      screen does exist would leave the permission ungrantable from inside
      the app - the one permission with no dialog of its own.
    -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
            <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
        </intent>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
            <category android:name="android.intent.category.HOME" />
        </intent>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.settings.USAGE_ACCESS_SETTINGS" />
        </intent>
    </queries>

    <application
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Harbor">
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/app_name"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Harbor"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"
            android:launchMode="singleTask">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />

                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!--
              Where a provider sign-in comes home to.

              Google and Microsoft take the browser, not the app: a password
              field on a screen the app drew is the exact shape of a phishing
              page, and both providers refuse to be loaded in a WebView for
              that reason. So the browser does it and hands back here, with the
              tokens in the URL fragment.

              singleTask above is what makes that land in the running app
              through onNewIntent rather than starting a second copy of Harbor
              on top of the first. The scheme has to match
              SupabaseClient.REDIRECT exactly; change one and the sign-in ends
              on a page that says it cannot be found.
            -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />

                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />

                <data android:scheme="harbor" android:host="auth" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          The cue surface. Its own task and excluded from recents: it is a
          moment, not a place you navigate back to. Showing over the lock
          screen is set in code, so it can be version-guarded.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".cue.CueActivity"
            android:exported="false"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:excludeFromRecents="true"
            android:showWhenLocked="true"
            android:turnScreenOn="true"
            android:taskAffinity=".cue"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Harbor" />

        <!--
          Google Play services delivers activity transitions here via a
          PendingIntent, with no service of ours running. exported="false":
          nothing outside the app may send us a fabricated transition.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".sensing.TransitionReceiver"
            android:exported="false" />

        <!--
          Does no work: it exists so the process is not cached, and therefore
          not frozen, so the receiver above can actually be woken. See
          sensing/SensingService.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".sensing.SensingService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="health" />

        <!--
          The settle timer. A walk that has just ended cannot fire yet — the
          policy wants ninety seconds of stillness first — and Play services
          has no further transition to send while somebody stays still, so
          this is what comes back to ask again. See sensing/SettleAlarm.

          No permission: the alarm is inexact (setAndAllowWhileIdle), which is
          deliberate — SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM is not worth spending on a
          ninety-second timer.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".sensing.SettleReceiver"
            android:exported="false" />

        <receiver
            android:name=".sensing.BootReceiver"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED" />
            </intent-filter>
            <!--
              A new build breaks sensing exactly as a reboot does, and more
              quietly. Participants are handed builds mid-week; without this
              their phone stops noticing walks until somebody opens Harbor by
              hand. Delivered to the app being replaced, so no data scheme is
              needed.
            -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!--
          "How is life right now", as a home-screen widget. exported="true":
          the widget host (the launcher) is a different app and has to be able
          to bind to this. See widget/WeatherWidget.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".widget.WeatherWidgetReceiver"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.appwidget.action.APPWIDGET_UPDATE" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.appwidget.provider"
                android:resource="@xml/weather_widget_info" />
        </receiver>
    </application>

</manifest>